# Rust, and why this source package does not build on a stock Debian 13 or
# Ubuntu 24.04 buildd
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the first question a Debian maintainer asks about a Rust package, so
# it is answered here rather than buried in README.Debian.
#
# The workspace pins the toolchain twice, and the two pins fail differently.
#
#   1. rust-toolchain.toml says channel = "1.98.1". That file is read by
#      rustup's proxy binaries and by nothing else — /usr/bin/cargo from
#      Debian's `cargo` package ignores it completely. It does bite when the
#      build host happens to have rustup installed: the proxy then tries to
#      *download* 1.98.1, and on a network-isolated builder that fails with a
#      message about a missing toolchain that looks nothing like the real
#      problem. debian/rules defaults RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN to stable unless the
#      caller set one (`?=`): that defuses it, and a CI container that
#      installed exactly 1.98.1 can still name that toolchain instead.
#      packaging/PKGBUILD does the same.
#
#   2. Cargo.toml says rust-version = "1.98.1". Cargo enforces that one itself,
#      with any rustc, and refuses the build outright: "package `fxsound-app
#      v0.3.0` cannot be built because it requires rustc 1.98.1 or newer".
#      Exporting RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN does nothing about it. Neither does removing
#      rust-toolchain.toml.
#
# Debian 13 (trixie) ships rustc 1.85; Ubuntu 24.04 (noble) ships 1.75, with
# rustc-1.80 available alongside it. 1.85 is the first release to stabilise
# edition 2024, which this workspace also requires — so trixie clears the
# edition gate and still trips over the rust-version floor, and noble clears
# neither. Until a rustc >= 1.98 reaches the archive (backports, or a later
# release), the honest position is that this package is built against a
# toolchain that is not the distribution's: rustup, `apt install rustup`, or a
# container image. The build dependency below is therefore written with a
# version that stock trixie cannot satisfy. That is deliberate: an unversioned
# `rustc` would let apt install a toolchain that then fails deep inside cargo
# with a message the person reading the log has to decode.
#
# Lowering rust-version in Cargo.toml to match the distribution's rustc is the
# obvious-looking fix and it is not one: the floor is there because the code
# uses edition-2024 and 1.98 language features, so the build fails later and
# with worse errors instead of failing at the gate.
#
# The second question: this package vendors nothing and resolves crates from
# crates.io through Cargo.lock. That is fine for a locally built .deb, a PPA or
# a third-party repository, and it is NOT acceptable for an upload to the
# Debian archive, which needs every dependency as a librust-*-dev package and a
# dh-cargo build. Packaging several hundred crates is a separate project; this
# tree does not pretend otherwise. See README.Debian for the `cargo vendor`
# route, which gets as far as an offline, self-contained build without
# attempting the archive's crate-per-package model.
Source: fxsound-linux
Section: sound
Priority: optional
Maintainer: FxSound Linux port contributors <blackixxce12@users.noreply.github.com>
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
 pkg-config,
# pipewire-sys links against libpipewire and runs bindgen over its headers.
 libpipewire-0.3-dev,
# libclang-dev is not optional: bindgen loads libclang.so at build time and
# fails with "Unable to find libclang" without it. A build only appears to work
# without it on a machine that has clang installed for something else.
 libclang-dev,
# See the note at the top of this file before touching these two.
 rustc (>= 1.98.1~),
 cargo (>= 1.98.1~),
Standards-Version: 4.7.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no
Homepage: https://github.com/blackixxce12/fxsound-linux
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/blackixxce12/fxsound-linux.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/blackixxce12/fxsound-linux

Package: fxsound-linux
Architecture: any
# Most of the graphics stack is opened with dlopen() rather than linked, so
# dpkg-shlibdeps cannot see it and every one of those libraries has to be named
# here by hand. `objdump -p target/release/fxsound` lists only libpipewire-0.3,
# libgcc_s, libm and libc; everything below that line is a string inside the
# binary that libloading resolves at startup. Drop one of them and the package
# installs cleanly and then fails to open a window, which is the worst of both
# worlds.
#
# ${shlibs:Depends} covers libc6, libgcc-s1 and libpipewire-0.3-0 with proper
# version bounds, so those are deliberately not repeated here.
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
# The daemon, not just the client library: FxSound creates a virtual sink in
# the PipeWire graph and has nothing to do without one.
 pipewire (>= 1.0),
# dlopen: libEGL.so.1 / libGL.so.1, through glutin.
 libegl1,
 libgl1,
# dlopen: libwayland-client.so.0 / libwayland-egl.so.1, through winit.
 libwayland-client0,
 libwayland-egl1,
# dlopen: libxkbcommon.so.0 and libxkbcommon-x11.so.0, for keyboard handling on
# both backends.
 libxkbcommon0,
 libxkbcommon-x11-0,
# dlopen: the X11 half of winit. eframe is built with both the `wayland` and
# `x11` features, so an X11 or XWayland session uses these and a Wayland
# session does not. They are Depends rather than Recommends because the
# fallback happens at runtime, inside the binary, and a package that works only
# on one of the two session types would be a surprise.
 libx11-6,
 libx11-xcb1,
 libxcursor1,
 libxi6,
 libxrender1,
# dlopen: libdbus-1.so.3, for the StatusNotifierItem tray and for desktop
# notifications.
 libdbus-1-3,
Recommends:
# Switching the session's default sink is a session-manager operation; without
# one, FxSound creates its sink and nothing moves onto it.
 wireplumber,
# Native file dialogs for preset import and export, through the portal. The
# portal itself only routes the request: a *backend* has to implement the file
# chooser, and wlroots' own backend does not. Any of the three desktop ones
# will do; the GTK one is the smallest and works under every compositor.
 xdg-desktop-portal,
 xdg-desktop-portal-gtk | xdg-desktop-portal-kde | xdg-desktop-portal-gnome,
# The D-Bus interface, the tray and notifications all need a session bus. Every
# desktop session starts one, but a bare compositor started from a tty does
# not — dbus-user-session gives it one through systemd --user.
 dbus-user-session,
# egui embeds its own base faces, so this is not required to start — it is what
# lets the window fall back to a system face for a script the embedded fonts do
# not cover.
 fontconfig,
Suggests:
# Only relevant if something on the machine still speaks PulseAudio.
 pipewire-pulse,
Provides: fxsound
Conflicts: fxsound
Replaces: fxsound
Description: system-wide audio enhancement for PipeWire
 FxSound processes everything the machine plays — a ten-band equalizer plus
 fidelity, ambience, surround, dynamic boost and bass — by presenting itself to
 PipeWire as a virtual sink that applications play into and forwarding the
 processed result to the real device. Pointing it at a microphone instead runs
 a separate voice chain (denoise, high-pass, expander, EQ, de-esser, compressor,
 limiter) and publishes a virtual source.
 .
 This is a native Linux port of the Windows application, written in Rust with
 egui. It is a fork rather than a wrapper: the GUI, the device layer and the
 persistence are new, but the DSP is ported line by line from the original C,
 so a preset voiced on Windows sounds the same here. The factory and bonus
 .fac presets are installed alongside it and are the upstream files unchanged.
 .
 A Wayland client cannot grab global shortcuts, so the keyboard bindings live
 in the compositor and call back into the running instance over a control
 socket; fxsound(1) documents the command line that makes that work.
